<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 98 0 R
/E (The theory of the harmony of the spheres has no precise originator: we know that it lies at the heart of Pythagorean doctrine, and that it arises from the concordance between the numerical structure of the seven strings of the heptachord and the system of the seven planets. According to Pythagorean doctrine, the Earth is a sphere at the center of the universe. The Sun, the Moon, and the planets revolve around it in concentric spheres, each fixed to its own sphere. According to Theon of Smyrna, it is precisely the Pythagoreans who first define the concept of the celestial sphere.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
177 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 98 0 R
/E (The movements of these celestial bodies create music that spreads through the air: each planet sounds at a different frequency, determined by the length of its orbit. These orbits are governed by universal harmony, such that the totality of the music produced is concordant and in harmony. According to tradition, only Pythagoras himself was capable of hearing this music.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
178 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 98 0 R
/E (The orbits of the celestial bodies form an enormous lyre with curved strings. It is difficult to determine whether this was a genuine scientific theory or a poetic artifact. Aristotle considered it a fantasy, and yet Kepler was fascinated by it two thousand years later.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
