/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (W.K.C. Guthrie notes that "the real Pythagoras is today more or less totally inaccessible." G.E.R. Lloyd emphasizes that there is insufficient evidence that he was a mathematician, a shaman, or a purely charismatic figure. What we know of Pythagoras's teachings comes to us principally through Plato and Aristotle, who however do not directly attribute the technical details of the astronomical theories to Pythagoras himself, citing "the Pythagoreans" in general.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
256 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (A fundamental principle of Pythagorean cosmology emerges from Aristotle, who cites Alcmaeon of Croton: all divine things must be in motion. Therefore, even the sphere of the fixed stars, though it moves very slowly, must possess its own proper motion.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
258 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (Mathematics is for the Pythagoreans sacred and enriching, and represents the method of access to ultimate truth. Two visions of the world thus stand in opposition: the Ionians against the Pythagoreans, the materialists against the mathematicians, and further: things in relation against the relations themselves, atoms against patterns, substance against form, and structure against function.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
